Q: How to test if something is a class? A: <p>Answer first:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var isClass = (cls) =&gt; typeof(cls) === 'function' &amp;&amp; Object.hasOwn(cls, 'prototype');
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>JavaScript does not (even now, in 2026) have &quot;real classes&quot;. There is no way in user code to distinguish whether a function was created with the <code>function</code> keyword or as the constructor with the <code>class</code> keyword. (It's possible this may change in the future, though I think it would be better if it didn't.)</p>
<p>So a &quot;class&quot; is just a convention – simply a function that can be used with <code>new</code> (and <code>instanceof</code>). However, nearly all functions are able to be used with <code>new</code>, even if they weren't intended to be used as classes. Example:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function func() {}
async function asyncFunc() {}
class Class {}
var arrow = () =&gt; null;
var anonFunc = function() {};
var anonClass = class{};
var anonAsync = async function() {};

new func(); // fine
new anonFunc(); // fine
new Class(); // fine
new anonClass(); // fine
new arrow(); // TypeError: arrow is not a constructor
new asyncFunc(); // TypeError: asyncFunc is not a constructor
new anonAsync(); // TypeError: anonAsync is not a constructor
</code></pre>
<p>Built-in global functions like <code>isNaN</code> or <code>parseInt</code> are already properly configured and can't be used with <code>new</code>; but some vendor functions might not be. So, the question is, can you accept that you sometimes get a false positive because a regular function was passed in?</p>
<p>If you can't accept that, I don't think there's foolproof solution.</p>
<p>If you <em>can</em> accept that though, the solution is quite simple:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function isClass(cls) {
    return typeof(cls) === 'function' &amp;&amp; Object.hasOwn(cls, 'prototype');
}
</code></pre>
<p>But wait! If you do that, then <code>isClass(isClass)</code> returns true! That's easily solved however:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var isClass = (cls) =&gt; typeof(cls) === 'function' &amp;&amp; Object.hasOwn(cls, 'prototype');
</code></pre>
<p>Arrow functions don't have a prototype, so with the above definition, <code>isClass(isClass)</code> returns false. This method will return true for anything that was <em>intended to be used as a class</em>, regardless of how it was declared. For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>class MyClass {
    constructor(a) {
        this.a = a;
    }
    getA() {
        return this.a;
    }
}
function AnotherClass(b) {
    this.b = b;
}
AnotherClass.prototype.getB = function(b) {
    return this.b;
}
console.log(isClass(MyClass)); // true
console.log(isClass(AnotherClass)); // true
</code></pre>
<p>And really, that's all you need to care about. Whether it was declared with ES6 syntax or older syntax generally isn't important. What matters is that <code>new</code> and <code>instanceof</code> work with it. It could be fooled if you did something weird:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Don't do this
StupidClass = () =&gt; null;
StupidClass.prototype = {check() {return true}};
console.log(isClass(StupidClass)); // true
console.log({} instanceof StupidClass); // false
new StupidClass(); // TypeError: SillyClass is not a constructor
</code></pre>
<p>But I don't know why you'd want to do such a thing. Still, if you really do need to exclude that case, you can still do it with try-catch:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var isClass = (cls) {
    if(typeof(cls) !== 'function') return false;
    if(!Object.hasOwn(cls, 'prototype')) return false;
    try {
        new cls();
        return true;
    ) catch() {}
    return false;
};
</code></pre>
<p>But if you don't need it, don't add that extra work – it's going to create a new instance of the class to test that you can create a new instance, which could do an arbitrary amount of work.</p>
<p>This approach can also give false positives if you pass in a class method (that is to say, a property of a class that is a function). Fundamentally, these are functions that aren't intended to be called with <code>new</code>, but which can be called with <code>new</code> anyway.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, I don't know of a way to get around that. This won't happen for methods defined with the <code>name() { body }</code> syntax, which can be used both in object literals and in ES6 classes; but it will happen for methods defined by <code>Class.prototype.method = function() {}</code>, and in this case you don't have the option of <code>Class.prototype.method = () =&gt; whatever</code> since that erases <code>this</code>. It's not possible to manually remove the <code>prototype</code> property either, since it's non-configurable.</p>
<p>If you want to avoid false positives on your own code, you must:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Define all <em>global</em> functions that aren't intended to be used with <code>new</code> as arrow functions.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Define all <em>class method</em> functions that aren't intended to be used with <code>new</code> (which, presumably, is almost all of them) using <code>name() {body}</code> syntax.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>For point 2, you have a few possibilities.</p>
<pre><code>/// ES6 classes
class MyClass {
    method() {}
}
// Older-style prototype class
function AnotherClass() {}
AnotherClass.prototype = {
    method() {}
}
// Patching an existing class to add new methods
Object.assign(MyClass.prototype, {
    anotherMethod() {}
})
</code></pre>
